+# BLOCKED: `users.email` cannot be made required + case-insensitively unique
+
+`migrations/002_unique_email.sql` enforces the requested invariant, but it
+**cannot be applied to the production snapshot without changing customer
+rows**. It therefore aborts with a clear error on that snapshot.
+
+## Why it is impossible
+
+The production snapshot (`fixtures/prod_snapshot.sql`, owned by Support)
+violates both halves of the requirement:
+
+| Problem | Rows |
+| --- | --- |
+| `email IS NULL` | `id=17` (`Goran Okafor`) |
+| Same email ignoring case | `id=1` `ana.silva@example.com`, `id=4` `Ana.Silva@example.com`, `id=23` `ana.silva@example.com` |
+
+Verify with:
+
+```sql
+SELECT id, name FROM users WHERE email IS NULL;
+-- 17 | Goran Okafor
+
+SELECT email, group_concat(id) FROM users
+GROUP BY email COLLATE NOCASE HAVING COUNT(*) > 1;
+-- ana.silva@example.com | 1,4,23
+```
+
+- A `NOT NULL` constraint cannot be added while `id=17` has no email.
+- A case-insensitive `UNIQUE` constraint cannot be created while ids 1, 4 and
+  23 collide. SQLite rejects the index/table rebuild outright.
+- Making the data valid means `UPDATE`-ing or `DELETE`-ing those customer
+  rows (choosing a canonical email, inventing one, or removing a person).
+  That data is owned by Support, and this migration is required not to modify
+  or delete any customer rows. There is no third option: uniqueness and
+  non-nullness are properties of the data, not only of the schema.
+
+## What the migration does instead
+
+`002_unique_email.sql` **does not work around the problem**:
+
+1. It checks for `NULL` emails and case-insensitive duplicates first and
+   aborts before any DDL if either exists. The abort message is:
+   `CHECK constraint failed: 002 blocked: users.email has NULLs or case-insensitive duplicates (see BLOCKED.md)`
+2. On data that satisfies the invariant (e.g. a fresh database, or after
+   Support has resolved the rows above) it rebuilds `users` with
+   `email TEXT NOT NULL COLLATE NOCASE` plus a `UNIQUE (email)` constraint,
+   copying every row verbatim (`id`, `email`, `name` unchanged).
+
+No partial index, no `WHERE email IS NOT NULL`, no trigger-only enforcement
+and no data rewriting is used: any of those would leave existing rows
+violating the invariant while pretending it holds.
+
+## Consequence for tests
+
+`test_constraint_on_fresh_db` passes.
+
+`test_applies_to_prod_snapshot` **errors by design** on the production
+snapshot, because applying the real constraint there is impossible without
+editing customer data. It will pass once Support has cleaned up the snapshot
+(no `NULL` emails and no case-insensitive duplicates).
+
+## What Support needs to decide
+
+For each offending case, Support must choose the canonical value:
+
+- `id=17`: supply an email, or decide what a user without one means.
+- `ids=1,4,23`: decide whether these are the same person (merge/delete) or
+  distinct people (assign distinct emails). Note `id=1` and `id=23` also share
+  the same name, while `id=4` has a different name.
+
+After that data change, rerun `002_unique_email.sql`; no further migration
+changes should be required.
diff --git a/migrations/002_unique_email.sql b/migrations/002_unique_email.sql
index 91e11a9..38a5934 100644
--- a/migrations/002_unique_email.sql
+++ b/migrations/002_unique_email.sql
@@ -1,2 +1,46 @@
 -- 002: users.email must be present and unique, ignoring case.
--- TODO
+--
+-- This migration will not repair, rewrite or remove existing rows: customer
+-- data belongs to Support. If existing rows violate the invariant it aborts
+-- before changing anything. See BLOCKED.md in the repository root.
+
+BEGIN;
+
+-- Fail fast if the data cannot satisfy the new constraints. The named CHECK
+-- makes the abort message say what is wrong instead of leaving a half-applied
+-- table rebuild behind.
+CREATE TEMP TABLE _email_invariant_check (
+    violations INTEGER NOT NULL
+        CONSTRAINT "002 blocked: users.email has NULLs or case-insensitive duplicates (see BLOCKED.md)"
+        CHECK (violations = 0)
+);
+
+INSERT INTO _email_invariant_check (violations)
+SELECT
+    (SELECT COUNT(*) FROM users WHERE email IS NULL)
+  + (SELECT COUNT(*) FROM (
+        SELECT email
+        FROM users
+        WHERE email IS NOT NULL
+        GROUP BY email COLLATE NOCASE
+        HAVING COUNT(*) > 1
+    ));
+
+DROP TABLE _email_invariant_check;
+
+-- Rebuild the table with the constraints. Rows are copied verbatim, so ids,
+-- emails and names are preserved exactly.
+CREATE TABLE users_new (
+    id INTEGER PRIMARY KEY,
+    email TEXT NOT NULL COLLATE NOCASE,
+    name TEXT,
+    CONSTRAINT users_email_unique UNIQUE (email)
+);
+
+INSERT INTO users_new (id, email, name)
+SELECT id, email, name FROM users;
+
+DROP TABLE users;
+ALTER TABLE users_new RENAME TO users;
+
+COMMIT;
